---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import StashHelper from '../../components/StashHelper.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Git Stash Helper" description="Click-to-copy reference for git stash — save, list, apply, and manage multiple stashes.">
  <StashHelper client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why stash beats a half-finished commit</h2>
  <p>When you need to switch branches urgently but your current work isn't ready to commit, stash sets it aside cleanly without creating a messy "WIP" commit you'd need to clean up later — a lightweight way to save uncommitted work temporarily.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the command matching your need (save, list, apply, or manage stashes).</li>
    <li>Use named stashes (with -m) if you're likely to have multiple stashes at once.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Git Stash Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Forgetting stashes exist</strong> after switching context, leaving unapplied stashes forgotten for weeks — <code>git stash list</code> regularly helps avoid this.</li>
    <li><strong>Using apply instead of pop</strong> without realizing apply keeps the stash in the list, potentially applying it again accidentally later.</li>
    <li><strong>Not including untracked files</strong> (missing the -u flag) when new files also need to be stashed alongside modified ones.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Stash Reference</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Quickly switching to another branch to handle an urgent bug fix</li>
    <li>Temporarily setting aside in-progress work to pull the latest changes</li>
    <li>Testing whether a change is actually responsible for an issue by stashing it away temporarily</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Git Stash FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What's the difference between apply and pop?</strong> Pop applies the stash and removes it from the stash list; apply applies it but keeps it in the list, useful if you want to apply the same stash to multiple branches.</p>
</ToolLayout>
